# Stand-in values for the variables Flux substitutes into the manifests.
#
# WHAT THIS IS FOR. `clusters/` carries `${VAR}` placeholders that Flux fills
# from secrets OpenTofu writes - bucket names, database identifiers, node
# addresses. On disk a manifest therefore holds the string "${STATE_DB_NODEPORT}"
# where a port number belongs, and kubeconform cannot check a manifest it
# cannot parse. Substituting these first means what gets validated is the
# shape Flux will actually apply.
#
# WHY THE VALUES ARE OBVIOUSLY FAKE. Only their shape is checked: a nodePort
# has to be an integer, an endpoint list has to be a list. The real values are
# addresses and identifiers this repository deliberately keeps out of git, so
# anything real here would be a leak rather than a better test. Addresses come
# from RFC 5737's documentation ranges and hostnames from .invalid, which
# exist precisely so examples cannot collide with somebody's estate.
#
# WHY A FILE RATHER THAN THE WORKFLOW. They lived in a `env:` block in
# pr-validation.yml, which made adding one a change to a protected path - a
# patch for the operator to apply, for a list of fake values. Here, adding a
# substitution is an ordinary edit, and `task validate` reads the same file CI
# does rather than a second copy that drifts.
#
# tests/go/repo/flux_substitutions_test.go holds this list and the manifests to
# each other in both directions.

STATE_DB_NAMESPACE=state
STATE_DB_CLUSTER=state-db
STATE_DB_NAME=tfstate
STATE_DB_OWNER=tofu
STATE_DB_NODEPORT=30432
OBJECT_STORAGE_BUCKET=placeholder-bucket
OBJECT_STORAGE_ENDPOINT=https://example.invalid
RELEASE_REPOSITORY=ghcr.io/example/homelab
# The runner's two. Absent from the workflow's list until this file existed,
# so both substituted to the empty string and CI validated a manifest asking
# a scale set to register with no organization and read no secret.
RUNNER_GITHUB_CONFIG_URL=https://example.invalid/fork
RUNNER_SECRET_NAME=placeholder-runner-secret
ETCD_ENDPOINTS=["192.0.2.10","192.0.2.11","192.0.2.12"]
